<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Ararat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ararat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Ararat is a high volcanic mountain in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, near the borders of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The surrounding region had a large Armenian population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> relocated Armenian merchant communities from the Ararat region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-julfa">New Julfa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, where they played a key role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-ararat.jpg" alt="Mount Ararat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Սէրուժ Ուրիշեան (Serouj Ourishian) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_the_Yerevan_skyline_in_spring_(50mm).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
